  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nA weak upper level trough moving east over the Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley is supporting a cold front which exited to the southeast of \nthe area during the day Friday. The front is over WV and eastern KY \nas of late Friday, but moisture overrunning the boundary is still \nallowing for showers as far north as far southern OH.\n\nThe current upper trough will depart to the east tonight and the\nfront will slow down or stall in the vicinity of the southern\nAppalachians. This will be enough progress for drier air to arrive\nfrom the north, put an end to precip, and break up clouds. Another\nupper level trough will approach the Great Lakes Saturday night, and \noverrunning moisture will head northward again. At this point, it\nlooks like there won't be anything more than clouds showing up over\nthe ILN forecast area, with precip remaining to the south.\n\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nThe models are in generally good agreement with west northwest flow \ndominating aloft through the middle of next week from the northern \nRockies through the middle Atlantic region. The upper level flow \nwill then become more zonal across the northern half of the CONUS by \nthe end of the week. For the Ohio Valley, mainly dry weather can be \nexpected. Near normal temperatures on Sunday will cool off to about \n10 degrees below normal for early next week, thanks to Canadian \nsurface high pressure that will migrate from the northern/central \nPlains to the Ohio Valley by Tuesday. This surface high will then \nmove off to the western Atlantic by Wednesday, with a weak cold \nfront to pass through the Ohio Valley Thursday into Friday, although \nmoisture looks limited at this time. Temperatures will gradually\nwarm across the region after Tuesday, reaching back to somewhat \nabove normal by Friday. \n\nSensible weather will feature generally decreasing clouds across the \narea on Sunday. Mostly sunny skies will dominate north of I-70, with \nclouds hanging in near the KY border through early afternoon, thanks \nto a departing system to the southeast. Mostly clear nights and \nmostly sunny days will then be the rule from Sunday night through \nthe majority of the upcoming work week. The coolest temperatures \nwill be in place from Monday through Wednesday morning, with highs \nmainly in the 60s and lows in the upper 30s to lower 40s. Depending \nupon how low dew points can get preceding both Monday night and \nTuesday night, a few sheltered locations could dip into the mid 30s, \nwith some spotty frost possible. Temperatures will then moderate each\nday through Friday, with highs warming through the 70s and lows in \nthe mid to upper 40s.",
